
There are standard state issues that were not addressed in the
original double annihilation procedure. This can be treated
properly by providing a harmonic constraint to keep the disappearing
particle in the binding site. The best description of the technology
can be found in J. Hermans & L. Wang, J. Am. Chem. Soc., 119, 2707-2714
(1997). An analytical correction is made for use of the constraint -
see Table 5 in this paper. The constraint is only used for the host-guest
complex; it is not needed for the unbound guest.

To permit such constrained calculations with BOSS 3.8, I have modified
the harmonic constraint code and input so it can handle either a bond
constraint (as before) and now the constraint of an atom to a fixed
XYZ point in space:

In the Z-matrix file:
C      ANY HARMONIC CONSTRAINTS FOR DISTANCES OR ATOMS - 1 PER LINE:
C      ATOM I, ATOM J, R0, K0, K1, K2
C        I4      I4       4F10.4
C      E CONSTRAINT = K0*(R(I,J)-R0)^2 FOR THE REFERENCE SOLUTE -
C      K1 AND K2 ARE USED ANALOGOUSLY FOR THE TWO PERTURBED SOLUTES.
C      IF ATOM J = 9999, THIS IS A POSITION CONSTRAINT FOR ATOM I
C      WITH R0 = FORCE CONSTANT AND K0, K1, K2 = X, Y, Z COORDINATES

Note that we use K*(R-R0)**2, while Hermans uses (K/2)*(R-R0)**2. So,
his entry in Table 5 for using K = 10 would be the same as our K = 5.
ALso, one wants to have IRECNT = 0 for such calculations to avoid 
recentering the solutes after each run since the X, Y, Z origin of
the atom constraint is fixed.

I am currently rerunning the Nora/Erin FEP calculations for disappearing
F- in the calixarene host and bound to one & two water molecules in chloroform.
The relevant part of the calixarene Z-matrix in that case is
                    Harmonic Constraints follow   (2I4,4F10.4)
01149999     2.5       0.177    -2.572     2.068
                    Variable Bond Angles follow   (I4)

